LLMs - Encoding LLMs vs Vector Embeddings

    There are 2 different types of LLMs

    1. Auto regressive LLMs -> PREDICTS FUTURE TOKENS FROM THE PAST INPUT
        a.  Trained to take an Input token and to predict next token that should come after this Input sequence
        b.  It's regressive and it looks bac over the input and predicts what should come next. It's predicts one
            token i.e Just the next token. The trick is we feed in the new Input with the
            (new token which is generated + old input). It just generates the next token and so on repeated again
            and again.
        c.  That's what an Auto-Regressive LLMs does. The reason it does is because that's what it trained to do so.

    2. Auto-encoding LLMs or Embedding model or Vector Embedding Models (PRODUCT OUTPUT BASED ON THE FULL INPUT)
        a.  Application include sentiment Analysis and classification
        b.  Also used to calculate "Vector Embedding" represents an input as a list of number - i.e a vector
        c.  Examples include BERT from Google and OpenAIEmbedding from OpenAI

---------------
Tokens vs Vectors

    Token -> Input
    Vectors -> Output

    Vectors:
        These vectors mathematically represents the meaning of an Input
        1.  Can represents a character, a token, a word, an entire document, or something abstract.
        2.  typically have hundreds or Thousands of dimensions
        3.  Represents an understanding of the input; similar inputs are close to each other
            > Points that are close to each other have similar meaning even thought the word themselves are completely
              different.
            > The model is trained so that it produces these number that have that these properties.
            > There is another way that these vectors represents meaning of their inputs and it's bit subtle and it
              was first discovered before transformers and before the LLM and this algorithm is called (WORD TO VEC)
        4. Vector Match
            The famous examples:
                1.  (King - Man + Women) -> Queen
                    If we do this vector math for (King - Man + Women) the closest point for this will be Queen
                2.  (Paris - France + England) -> The closest point will be London.

